**3 — `ApplyVariableNames.java:210` — `name.toUpperCase().equals(name)` allocates a whole String to test for all-uppercase (efficiency)**

```java
if (name.toUpperCase().equals(name)) {
```

`toUpperCase()` allocates a new `String` on every call. Replace with a char scan:

```java
if (name.chars().allMatch(c -> Character.toUpperCase(c) == c)) {
```

No allocation; equivalent semantics. This runs per `SSAVar` across every method in the decompiled APK.

---

**4 — `ClassNode.java:559-592` — Four nearly-identical linear-search for-loops (simplification)**

```java
public FieldNode searchField(FieldInfo field) {       // line 559
    for (FieldNode f : fields) { if (f.getFieldInfo().equals(field)) return f; } return null; }
public FieldNode searchFieldByNameAndType(FieldInfo field) {  // line 568
    for (FieldNode f : fields) { if (f.getFieldInfo().equalsNameAndType(field)) return f; } return null; }
public FieldNode searchFieldByName(String name) {     // line 577
    for (FieldNode f : fields) { if (f.getName().equals(name)) return f; } return null; }
public FieldNode searchFieldByShortId(String shortId) { // line 586
    for (FieldNode f : fields) { if (f.getFieldInfo().getShortId().equals(shortId)) return f; } return null; }
```

Each is the same pattern with a different predicate. Replace each with `ListUtils.firstOrNull`:

```java
public FieldNode searchField(FieldInfo field) {
    return ListUtils.firstOrNull(fields, f -> f.getFieldInfo().equals(field)); }
public FieldNode searchFieldByNameAndType(FieldInfo field) {
    return ListUtils.firstOrNull(fields, f -> f.getFieldInfo().equalsNameAndType(field)); }
public FieldNode searchFieldByName(String name) {
    return ListUtils.firstOrNull(fields, f -> f.getName().equals(name)); }
public FieldNode searchFieldByShortId(String shortId) {
    return ListUtils.firstOrNull(fields, f -> f.getFieldInfo().getShortId().equals(shortId)); }
```

---

**5 — `AnnotationGen.java:92-105` and `125-132` — duplicate iterator-join pattern (simplification)**

Both `formatAnnotation` (annotation value list) and `addThrows` (throws type list) contain the same boilerplate:

```java
for (Iterator<X> it = list.iterator(); it.hasNext();) {
    X x = it.next();
    // process x
    if (it.hasNext()) { code.add(", "); }
}
```

The pattern appears twice verbatim. Extract a private helper `addCommaSeparated(Iterable<T>, Consumer<T>)` or replace with a boolean-first-flag idiom shared between the two loops.

---

**6 — `PrepareForCodeGen.java:316` — `Stream.of(three-lists).flatMap` for a simple min-line search (simplification)**

```java
int minLine = Stream.of(cls.getMethods(), cls.getInnerClasses(), cls.getFields())
    .flatMap(Collection::stream)
    .filter(mth -> !mth.contains(AFlag.DONT_GENERATE))
    .filter(mth -> mth.getSourceLine() != 0)
    .mapToInt(LineAttrNode::getSourceLine)
    .min().orElse(0);
```

This creates four `Stream` objects and a `OptionalInt` to compute one integer. A plain loop with a running minimum is O(n) with zero allocation:

```java
int minLine = 0;
for (LineAttrNode node : Iterables.concat(cls.getMethods(), cls.getInnerClasses(), cls.getFields())) {
    if (!node.contains(AFlag.DONT_GENERATE) && node.getSourceLine() != 0) {
        int line = node.getSourceLine();
        if (minLine == 0 || line < minLine) minLine = line;
    }
}
```

(Or three separate loops over each collection if Guava's `Iterables` is not available.)

